1
00:00:00,170 --> 00:00:03,591
The difference between a useless
AI answer and a brilliant

2
00:00:03,591 --> 00:00:06,481
one is usually four small
parts in your message.

3
00:00:06,481 --> 00:00:08,745
Most people only use one of them.

4
00:00:08,745 --> 00:00:12,517
We're going to build a prompt from
scratch, one block at a time,

5
00:00:12,517 --> 00:00:15,409
and watch the answer get
better with every block.

6
00:00:15,409 --> 00:00:19,076
And at the end there's a trick that
basically writes the prompt for you.

7
00:00:20,188 --> 00:00:24,512
Here's where most people start. "Write
a birthday message for my mum."

8
00:00:24,512 --> 00:00:27,971
And you get back something
like, "Thank you for the love,

9
00:00:27,971 --> 00:00:29,192
the patience,

10
00:00:29,192 --> 00:00:32,940
and all the times you believed in
me before I believed in myself."

11
00:00:32,940 --> 00:00:36,909
It's fine. It's also a greeting
card from a petrol station.

12
00:00:36,909 --> 00:00:38,894
It could be for anyone's mum.

13
00:00:38,894 --> 00:00:40,678
That's because you only gave it one block.

14
00:00:41,657 --> 00:00:45,669
Block one. The task.
What you want it to do.

15
00:00:45,669 --> 00:00:50,252
"Write a birthday message" is a
task. Everybody gets this block.

16
00:00:50,252 --> 00:00:51,862
It's the other three that make the magic.

17
00:00:52,714 --> 00:00:55,170
Block two. The context.

18
00:00:55,170 --> 00:00:58,111
The stuff that's in your
head that the AI can't see.

19
00:00:58,111 --> 00:00:59,801
"My mum's turning sixty.

20
00:00:59,801 --> 00:01:03,574
She just retired after thirty
years as a nurse. She's funny,

21
00:01:03,574 --> 00:01:07,457
a bit sarcastic, and she'd
cringe at anything too soppy."

22
00:01:07,457 --> 00:01:09,072
Now look at the answer.

23
00:01:09,072 --> 00:01:11,819
"The NHS will have to find
someone else to give that

24
00:01:11,819 --> 00:01:14,514
look to patients who say
'it's probably nothing.'"

25
00:01:14,514 --> 00:01:15,835
Suddenly it's about her.

26
00:01:15,835 --> 00:01:22,025
The nursing, the retirement,
the sense of humor. Same AI.

27
00:01:22,025 --> 00:01:23,426
You just let it into your head.

28
00:01:24,339 --> 00:01:29,543
Block three. The rules.
Length, tone, what to avoid.

29
00:01:29,543 --> 00:01:33,546
"Keep it under fifty words. It's
going in a card, so no emojis.

30
00:01:33,546 --> 00:01:34,661
Don't say 'queen.'"

31
00:01:34,661 --> 00:01:38,591
You'd be surprised how much the AI
wants to call your mum a queen.

32
00:01:38,591 --> 00:01:41,209
The rules stop the stuff
you'd delete anyway.

33
00:01:41,209 --> 00:01:43,431
Now it's short and card-ready:

34
00:01:43,431 --> 00:01:45,626
"Thirty years of bossing doctors around,

35
00:01:45,626 --> 00:01:47,445
and now you're free to boss us full-time."

36
00:01:48,429 --> 00:01:51,494
Block four. Show it what good looks like.

37
00:01:51,494 --> 00:01:53,894
This is the one almost nobody uses,

38
00:01:53,894 --> 00:01:56,169
and it's probably the most powerful.

39
00:01:56,169 --> 00:01:58,708
Paste in a few texts
you've actually sent her,

40
00:01:58,708 --> 00:02:02,653
like "Tell Dad the fence he
fixed has fallen over again."

41
00:02:02,653 --> 00:02:06,622
Look what came back: "Now you're
stuck at home with Dad full time.

42
00:02:06,622 --> 00:02:10,338
Toughest shift yet." It
picked up Dad from my texts.

43
00:02:10,338 --> 00:02:12,281
I never asked it to mention him.

44
00:02:12,281 --> 00:02:14,980
An example beats a description every time.

45
00:02:14,980 --> 00:02:19,189
Telling it "be casual" is vague.
Showing it how you actually talk...

46
00:02:19,189 --> 00:02:19,989
that's a blueprint.

47
00:02:20,809 --> 00:02:25,290
Put them together, and compare. The
first message could be for anyone.

48
00:02:25,290 --> 00:02:27,768
The new one could only be for your mum.

49
00:02:27,768 --> 00:02:30,498
That's the real test of a
good prompt, by the way.

50
00:02:30,498 --> 00:02:34,119
Could the answer only be for
you? If it could be for anyone,

51
00:02:34,119 --> 00:02:37,084
you're missing a block. Usually context.

52
00:02:37,084 --> 00:02:40,716
And as someone who works with AI
every day as a software engineer,

53
00:02:40,716 --> 00:02:45,321
I'll be honest. I still catch myself
writing lazy one-block prompts.

54
00:02:45,321 --> 00:02:46,994
Then I wonder why the answer is lazy.

55
00:02:47,885 --> 00:02:52,280
Okay, the trick I promised. If you
don't know what context to give,

56
00:02:52,280 --> 00:02:56,297
make the AI figure it out.
Add this line to any prompt.

57
00:02:56,297 --> 00:02:59,741
"Before you answer, ask me
any questions you need."

58
00:02:59,741 --> 00:03:04,270
Instead of guessing, it came
back with, "What tone? How long?

59
00:03:04,270 --> 00:03:08,338
Is it a milestone birthday?
Card, text or speech?

60
00:03:08,338 --> 00:03:13,293
How do you usually sign off?" You
answer, and boom. All four blocks,

61
00:03:13,293 --> 00:03:15,357
filled in, and you barely had to think.

62
00:03:16,239 --> 00:03:20,079
So. The task. The context. The rules.

63
00:03:20,079 --> 00:03:22,589
And an example of what good looks like.

64
00:03:22,589 --> 00:03:24,915
You don't need all four every time.

65
00:03:24,915 --> 00:03:28,356
Asking what time it is in Tokyo
doesn't need your life story.

66
00:03:28,356 --> 00:03:32,452
But anything personal, anything you'd
actually send to a real person,

67
00:03:32,452 --> 00:03:33,343
give it the blocks.

68
00:03:34,230 --> 00:03:36,129
Quick thing you might notice now.

69
00:03:36,129 --> 00:03:39,834
Ask the same prompt twice and
you get two different answers.

70
00:03:39,834 --> 00:03:43,058
Is that a bug? Nope. Next video,

71
00:03:43,058 --> 00:03:46,440
I'll show you why AI rolls the
dice on purpose. I gotchu.

